-- title:
--   The discrete quadratic form over an arbitrary lattice
-- statement:
--   Let $L \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be a lattice, $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ a region, $R_0 > 0$ a separation threshold, $\omega$ a kernel on $\mathbb{R}^d \times \mathbb{R}^d$ with values in $[0,\infty]$, and $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$. The **discrete quadratic form** of $f$ on $S$ relative to $L$ is the sum, over ordered pairs of points of $S \cap L$ at distance more than $R_0$, of the squared increment weighted by the kernel:
--
--   $$\mathcal{E}^{L}_{S,R_0}[\omega, f] \;=\; \sum_{\substack{x,y \,\in\, S\cap L \\ \|x-y\| > R_0}} \big(f(x) - f(y)\big)^2\, \omega(x,y).$$
--
--   The module fixes this one definition; its intended use is with $L = h\mathbb{Z}^d$, the rescaled lattice, where the surrounding argument identifies $x \mapsto hx$ as the map carrying $\mathbb{Z}^d$ onto $h\mathbb{Z}^d$, multiplying distances by $h$ and hence multiplying a jump kernel of order $-d-\alpha$ by $h^{-d-\alpha}$.
--
--   Isolating the lattice as a parameter is what lets a single discrete inequality be quoted at every mesh: the statement proved on $\mathbb{Z}^d$ transfers to $h\mathbb{Z}^d$ by rescaling the kernel, without re-proving the estimate at each scale.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The sum is a `tsum` over the subtype of pairs satisfying the constraint, valued in $\mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}^{\infty}$, where unconditional summability is automatic and no convergence hypothesis is needed; the squared increment enters through `ENNReal.ofReal`, which is the identity on nonnegative reals. Each unordered pair is therefore counted twice, once in each order. No hypothesis is imposed on $L$, $S$, $R_0$ or $\omega$ — the definition is meaningful for any of them, the lattice interpretation being supplied at the use sites.
